From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Graham Ollis <plicease@cpan.org>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 18:10:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Prototype the varargs functions for Open Watcom's calling
 convention

fatal(), warn() and deb() are defined with <varargs.h> (va_alist,
va_dcl), which Open Watcom expands to a variadic definition, taking every
argument on the stack. Perl 3 calls them with no declaration in scope,
and with no prototype Watcom's default register convention passes the
first arguments in AX, DX, BX and CX. The functions then formatted
whatever was on the stack: every die and warn message was garbage, and a
die from some places (an undefined subroutine, for one) crashed DOS.
Microsoft C passed everything on the stack, so the historical port never
saw this.

perl.h now declares all three with a variadic prototype under
__WATCOMC__.
---
 perl.h | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/perl.h b/perl.h
index 1e1a256..0779473 100644
--- a/perl.h
+++ b/perl.h
@@ -477,6 +477,17 @@ double str_gnum();
 #endif /* CRIPPLED_CC */
 EXT STR *Str;
 
+#ifdef __WATCOMC__
+/* fatal(), warn() and deb() are defined with <varargs.h>, which Open
+ * Watcom implements as variadic functions taking their arguments on the
+ * stack.  A call with no prototype in scope passes the first arguments in
+ * registers, so every caller must see these.  (perl16)
+ */
+int fatal(void *, ...);
+int warn(void *, ...);
+int deb(void *, ...);
+#endif
+
 #define GROWSTR(pp,lp,len) if (*(lp) < (len)) growstr(pp,lp,len)
 
 #ifndef MSDOS
